Dataset
The dataset contains single-dose warfarin pharmacokinetic observations from 32 healthy volunteers, with 251 plasma concentration measurements.
Available subject characteristics included:

body weight;
age;
sex;
administered dose;
sampling time;
plasma warfarin concentration.

The study included measurements extending from the absorption phase through 120 hours after administration. Sampling intensity varied between individuals, creating both richly sampled and relatively sparse concentration-time profiles.



Observed median concentrations and population and individual predictions over time. The shaded region represents the observed interquartile range.


The dataset is distributed as an educational example with MonolixSuite and was originally derived from clinical studies by O’Reilly and colleagues.
Access the Monolix warfarin dataset

Educational objectives
The main objectives were to gain practical experience with:

exploratory analysis of longitudinal PK data;
structural model selection;
residual-error model selection;
interindividual variability;
covariate screening and model building;
fixed allometric scaling;
SAEM convergence assessment;
goodness-of-fit diagnostics;
visual predictive checks;
random-effect and residual diagnostics;
nonparametric bootstrap uncertainty analysis;
recognizing overparameterization and weak identifiability.

Model-development workflow
I evaluated 15 candidate models representing combinations of:

one- and two-compartment disposition;
delayed and non-delayed absorption;
alternative residual-error models;
different random-effect structures;
weight and age covariate effects;
estimated and fixed allometric relationships;
alternative initial parameter values.

Model development was performed iteratively. Each modification was evaluated using parameter estimates, relative standard errors, information criteria, convergence traces, diagnostic plots, and biological interpretability.

Final population PK model
The selected model consisted of:

one disposition compartment;
first-order absorption;
an absorption lag time;
linear elimination;
lognormally distributed individual parameters;
interindividual variability on (k_a), (V), and (Cl);
fixed allometric effects of body weight;
an estimated age effect on clearance;
a combined additive and proportional residual-error model.

For individual (i), volume and clearance were represented as:
\[
V_i =
V_{\mathrm{pop}}
\left(\frac{WT_i}{70}\right)^1
\exp(\eta_{V,i})
\]
and
\[
Cl_i =
Cl_{\mathrm{pop}}
\left(\frac{WT_i}{70}\right)^{0.75}
\exp\left(\beta_{\mathrm{age}}\,
\frac{AGE_i-40}{10}\right)
\exp(\eta_{Cl,i}).
\]
The fixed exponents of 1 for volume and 0.75 for clearance reflect conventional allometric scaling assumptions.
The combined residual-error model was:
\[
y_{ij} =
\hat y_{ij} +
\left(a+b\hat y_{ij}\right)\varepsilon_{ij},
\qquad
\varepsilon_{ij}\sim\mathcal N(0,1).
\]

Model diagnostics
Population and individual predictions generally captured the concentration-time profiles, and individual predictions aligned closely with observed concentrations.



Observed warfarin concentrations compared with population and individual predictions.


The visual predictive check showed that the observed central tendency was generally represented by the model and that most observations remained within the simulated prediction interval.
However, the diagnostic analysis also identified limitations:

several residual-distribution tests rejected normality;
the clearance random effects departed from the assumed normal distribution;
a small number of individuals had large standardized clearance effects;
sparse or late-only sampling limited individual information for some subjects;
absorption variability remained imprecisely estimated.

These findings were retained as part of the model evaluation rather than hidden by selecting the most favorable diagnostics.



Individual weighted residuals plotted against time and individual predictions.

Calibration and validation
The model was calibrated using experimental measurements of VEGF- and hypoxia-responsive signaling, including:

VEGFR2 activation;
Akt and ERK phosphorylation;
calcium signaling;
eNOS activation and nitric oxide production;
Dll4, NICD, and Hes1 dynamics;
HIF1α, HIF2α, and VEGFA responses.

Independent datasets were used to evaluate predictions for Dll4, Akt, eNOS, ERK, and HIF signaling.
Identifiability and sensitivity analyses reduced the original set of 66 unknown parameters to 20 parameters selected for estimation, improving the feasibility and interpretability of model calibration.

My contribution
I led the computational development of this project. My contributions included:

conceptualization;
model construction and software implementation;
data curation;
parameter estimation and formal analysis;
identifiability, sensitivity, and uncertainty analyses;
model validation;
simulation design and visualization;
project administration;
preparation and revision of the manuscript.

Modeling approach
The model integrates:

VEGF-VEGFR2 signaling;
HGF-cMet signaling;
PI3K-Akt and ERK pathways;
calcium and nitric-oxide signaling;
hypoxia-dependent regulation;
PAK1 phosphorylation;
endothelial proliferation and permeability responses.

Structural and practical identifiability analyses were used to identify estimable parameter combinations. Global sensitivity analysis then selected the most influential unknown parameters for calibration.
To translate molecular signaling into functional predictions, the study introduced two model-derived quantities:

a proliferation index;
a permeability index.

These indices enabled systematic comparison of HGF, VEGF, and combined stimulation across doses and oxygen conditions.

Model evaluation
The model was calibrated and evaluated against experimental data from multiple sources under normoxia and hypoxia.
Key evaluation results included:

reproduction of time- and dose-dependent experimental trends;
validation against dose-response data not used in calibration;
Runs tests consistent with an absence of systematic residual bias, with an average p = 0.648;
bootstrap-based uncertainty quantification;
agreement with reported combined HGF-VEGF effects on downstream signaling.

Research experience
My research experience includes:

mechanistic modeling of Notch, HGF, VEGF, and HIF signaling;
angiogenesis and peripheral arterial disease;
cancer immunotherapy and quantitative oncology;
cerebral blood-flow and image-informed modeling;
machine learning/deep learning models for cerebral hemodynamics and cardiovascular data;
biomedical devices and physiological systems;
biomaterials and chronic-wound technologies.

How I approach modeling
I am especially interested in models that are:

mechanistically interpretable;
informed by experimental or clinical data;
transparent about uncertainty and limitations;
evaluated for numerical and predictive robustness;
designed to answer a clear scientific or therapeutic question.

For me, a model is most useful when it does more than reproduce observations: it should clarify assumptions, connect evidence across scales, and help guide the next scientific decision.

Scientific question
How do oxygen availability, HIF isoform dynamics, and PHD-mediated feedback interact to regulate endothelial responses during acute and prolonged hypoxia?
The model was designed to investigate:

graded sensitivity to oxygen availability;
the transition from an acute HIF1-dominated response to a prolonged HIF2-dominated response;
the relative contributions of HIF1 and HIF2 to VEGFA regulation;
the distinct effects of PHD2 and PHD3 inhibition.

Modeling approach
The model integrates oxygen-dependent hydroxylation and degradation of HIF proteins, nuclear signaling, transcriptional feedback, and VEGFA mRNA regulation.
The development workflow included:

literature-based model construction;
structural identifiability analysis;
practical identifiability analysis;
global parameter estimation using particle swarm optimization;
local parameter refinement;
uncertainty quantification;
validation against independent experimental datasets;
predictive simulations under new oxygen and inhibition scenarios.

My contribution
My work includes:

development and extension of mechanistic QSP models;
implementation and evaluation of therapeutic mechanisms;
parameterization using experimental and clinical literature;
numerical simulation and troubleshooting;
generation and qualification of virtual individuals;
selection and robustness assessment of virtual populations;
implementation of in silico clinical-trial workflows;
analysis of treatment-response and subgroup outcomes;
global sensitivity and potential biomarker analyses;
development of reproducible computational pipelines;
scientific visualization and communication of modeling results.

Current status
This is an active research project. The public description will be expanded when results and implementation details become appropriate for public disclosure.
Future updates may include:

